# =====================================================================
# Whole-scene .ftsl grammar (GPDA / EPEG, *generic* flavour)
# =====================================================================
# A faithful mirror of ftrace's hand-written front-end (src/ftsl.h:
# tokenize + Parser::parseTop / parseOneTopBlock / parseValue /
# parseBraceBody / parseRecord / parsePrefer).  Where the sibling
# `ftsl.epeg` is a *typed* grammar of six element kinds (loom's reader,
# the J3c "proving ground"), THIS grammar is the generic, uniform
# block/stmt/value structure ftrace's parser actually produces — the one
# the C++ front-end will adopt and the validation shim will diff against.
#
# KEY: ftrace's tokenizer is trivial — everything that isn't
# whitespace / a brace / a bracket / a comment / a quoted string is a
# single `Word` (so `0.5+0.5*sin(2*pi*8*u)`, `spectrum:gold`,
# `smooth_union`, `REC.chan` are each ONE token).  We reproduce that with
# a catch-all WORD terminal, plus NUMWORD / KVWORD carved off the front
# ONLY so the grammar can honour ftrace's parseValue *continuation rule*:
# after a value's first token, a value continues on numbers and `key=val`
# words but STOPS at a plain bareword (which begins the next statement's
# key).  Literals (`scene` isn't one — only `range`/`prefer`/`else`/
# `spectrum`/`=`) match by *value* against whatever token the lexer emits,
# so keywords need no special lexing.
# =====================================================================

start scene_file

# ---- skips + terminals (mirror ftrace's tokenize) -------------------
WS      = /[ \t\r]+/ @skip          # inter-token whitespace (ftrace skips \r too)
COMMENT = /#[^\n]*/ @skip           # `# …` to end of line
NEWLINE = /\n/                      # significant: delimits statements / blocks

STRING  = /"[^"]*"/                                 # quoted name / path / expression
# A wholly-parenthesised run — `(u)`, `(u,v)`, `(0.5+0.5*sin(2*pi*u))`.  Carved off
# the front of WORD ONLY so an array literal's *sample call* has a token type of its
# own: `[0 1](u)`.  ftrace's tokenizer deliberately does NOT treat parens as
# delimiters — that is exactly what keeps an expression value like
# `0.5+0.5*sin(2*pi*8*u)` a single token — so making `(` a delimiter would shatter
# every expression in the language.  A context-free terminal that matches a token
# which is ENTIRELY parenthesised (starts '(', ends ')', no delimiter between) names
# the call position without touching them: longest-match keeps expressions safe,
# since `(a+b)*c` is 7 chars of WORD against only 5 of PARENWORD, so WORD wins.
# Declared before KVWORD so the keyword-rebind form `(a=u)` lexes as a call rather
# than as a `key=val` value continuation.
#
# A paren group may contain SPACES (`f(0.5*u + 0.5*v)`, `gold(a=1, u=v)`).  That is why
# the interior class below allows ' ' and '\t' but the WORD class still does not: a space
# is only "inside a token" while parens are open.  The group is therefore matched
# BALANCED rather than by a greedy `[^)]*\)`, because a greedy run that may cross spaces
# would happily swallow `(a) roughness 0.2` up to a later `)` in a *different* statement —
# a silently wrong parse.  Balanced matching stops dead at the closing paren.
# Regexes cannot count, so nesting is written out to depth 4:
#     interior := [^\r\n(){}#"]                   (spaces yes, parens/braces no)
#     G1 := \( interior* \)   G(k+1) := \( (interior|Gk)* \)
# Depth is not a real ceiling: WORD below is `(G|wordchar)+` and a wordchar still matches
# a bare paren, so an expression nested deeper simply falls back to today's behaviour
# (outer parens consumed as ordinary chars, G covering the innermost spaced group).
#
# `[` and `]` ARE interior characters (they were excluded until 0.100.0), so a sample call
# can carry a COMPOSED coordinate whose driver is itself an array literal —
# `[0 1]([0.2 0.8](u))`, TODO.md's `coord = NAME | NUMBER | value`.  Without this the
# inner literal's brackets split the token and the outer literal reported the (very
# misleading) *unsaturated* error.  This does NOT weaken the balance guarantee above,
# which rests entirely on `)` and `(` staying OUT of the interior class: two paren groups
# on one line still cannot merge, because merging would have to consume the intervening
# `)` as an interior char.  Brackets inside the group are therefore captured but not
# balance-checked by the lexer -- the loader parses the argument text and reports an
# unbalanced one against the author's own source (see desugarNestedLiterals in ftsl.h),
# which gives a far better message than a token that silently fails to match.
PARENWORD = /\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\([^\r\n(){}#"]*\))*\))*\))*\)/
# A strtod-complete numeric literal (ftrace's isNumber): a value *continuation*.
# `0-1` / `1-2` are NOT numbers (strtod stops at the inner dash) — longest-match
# then makes them plain WORDs, exactly as ftrace's isNumber returns false.
NUMWORD = /[+-]?([0-9]+\.?[0-9]*|\.[0-9]+)([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?/
# A `key=value` word (`center=560`, `axis=x`): also a value continuation
# (ftrace continues on any word containing '=').  A lone `=` has no head, so it
# falls through to WORD and is matched by the `'='` literal — keeping the
# `NAME = KIND` header and record-override `slot = rhs` forms unambiguous.
KVWORD  = /[^ \t\r\n{}\[\]#"=]+=[^ \t\r\n{}\[\]#"]*/
# The catch-all bareword: any run of non-delimiter chars, where a BALANCED PAREN GROUP
# counts as one such "char" and may contain spaces.  Declared LAST so it loses
# longest-match ties to NUMWORD / KVWORD / STRING (numbers & k=v win).
#
# The group alternative is written FIRST because ECMAScript/Python regexes are
# leftmost-FIRST, not leftmost-longest: the engine must prefer "consume the whole
# balanced group" over "consume '(' as an ordinary char and stop at the next space".
# The second alternative is exactly the historical WORD class (parens included), so
# every token that lexed before still lexes identically — the group alternative can only
# ever EXTEND a token across a space that sits inside parens, which previously truncated
# the value mid-expression and raised "missing ')'".
#
# The group alternative is now character-for-character PARENWORD's body, brackets included
# (0.100.0), so a NAMED table's sample call can carry a composed array literal exactly as an
# inline literal's can: `reflect grid:ramp([0.2 0.8](u))`.  Only the group interior admits
# `[` / `]` — the fallback class below still excludes them, so a bracket OUTSIDE parens is
# a delimiter as it always was, and `REC.chan[2]` still stops the word at the `[`.  Two
# tokens on one line cannot merge for the same reason PARENWORD's cannot: merging would
# have to consume the intervening `)` as an interior char, and `)` is excluded.
WORD    = /(?:\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\((?:[^\r\n(){}#"]|\([^\r\n(){}#"]*\))*\))*\))*\)|[^ \t\r\n{}\[\]#"])+/

# ---- file = a list of top-level blocks (newlines optional between) ---
scene_file = item*
item       = NEWLINE | top_block

# parseOneTopBlock: prefer-construct, `NAME = range …` record, `spectrum … = v`,
# the unified `NAME = KIND { }` header, or the legacy `KIND ["name"] [sub] { }`.
top_block  = prefer_block | record_decl | spectrum_decl | assign_header | plain_header

# ---- prefer { … } else { … } ----------------------------------------
prefer_block = 'prefer' blank_lines block_list ('else' blank_lines block_list)*
block_list   = '{' item* '}'
blank_lines  = NEWLINE*

# ---- parametric record: `NAME = range LO HI [ <lines> ]` -------------
record_decl  = WORD '=' 'range' range_word* blank_lines record_body
range_word   = NUMWORD | KVWORD | STRING | WORD | PARENWORD
record_body  = '[' rec_item* ']'
rec_item     = NEWLINE | record_line
record_line  = WORD stop_word*                 # channel name + stop tokens
# A stop token, or an explicit `[ … ]` GROUP — the bracket rung of the generalized
# stop grammar's delimiter precedence ladder (whitespace binds tightest like `×`,
# comma looser like `+`, brackets are the ladder's parentheses).  Commas need no rule
# here: `,` is NOT one of ftrace's tokenizer delimiters, so `0 0 0, 1 1 1` already
# lexes as words with the comma riding along (`0,`) and the loader's ladder re-splits
# them paren-aware — which is precisely what keeps an expression like `clamp(x,0,1)`
# a single leaf.  Brackets DO delimit, so the group has to be spelled out; the reducer
# flattens it back into the word list with `[` / `]` marker words (no real word can
# ever equal one, since both are excluded from every terminal's character class).
# NEWLINE inside a group lets a long gradient be laid out over several lines, exactly
# as `selector` already allows for an array literal.
stop_word    = NUMWORD | KVWORD | STRING | WORD | PARENWORD | stop_group
stop_group   = '[' stop_item* ']'
stop_item    = NEWLINE | stop_word

# ---- spectrum decl: `spectrum "name" [subtype] = <value>` -----------
# The one block form with an `=` value instead of a brace body.
spectrum_decl = 'spectrum' STRING? subtype? '=' value

# ---- element headers -------------------------------------------------
# Unified `NAME = KIND [subtype] { body }` and legacy `KIND ["name"] [sub] { body }`.
assign_header = WORD '=' WORD subtype? brace_body
plain_header  = WORD STRING? subtype? brace_body
subtype       = WORD                           # a bareword subtype (light area / …)

# ---- brace body = newline-structured statements ---------------------
brace_body = '{' body_item* '}'
body_item  = NEWLINE | stmt
# ftrace takes *any* token as a statement key (`s.key = cur().text`), so a
# `palette { 0 spectrum:navy  1 spectrum:crimson … }` flat-list body (numeric
# "keys") parses the same as a normal `key value` line.
stmt       = key_tok value
key_tok    = WORD | NUMWORD | KVWORD

# parseValue: the record-override `= rhs [i]` form, or a normal value — a first
# token (any), then continuations (numbers / k=v / strings), an optional
# `[…]` bracket group, and an optional trailing nested `{ … }` block.
value       = override_val | normal_val
override_val = '=' rhs? selector?
rhs         = STRING | NUMWORD | KVWORD | WORD | PARENWORD
normal_val  = val_head? cont* selector? block?
val_head    = STRING | NUMWORD | KVWORD | WORD | PARENWORD
cont        = NUMWORD | KVWORD | STRING        # NOT a plain WORD (that ends the value)
# ONE production covers both jobs of `[ … ]` at a value site, because there is only
# ever one way to read a bracket group here and keeping them merged is what keeps the
# grammar unambiguous:
#   * the record-channel STOP SELECTOR, `REC.chan[2]` — a flat run of words that the
#     reducer folds back into the preceding dotted word; and
#   * an inline N-D ARRAY LITERAL, `[0 1](u)` / `[[0 1 2][3 4 5]](u,v)` — nesting is
#     the shape, and the trailing `axistuple` is the *sample call* that says at which
#     coordinates the array is read.  An uncalled array is legal to write but
#     `unsaturated`; that error lives in the loader, not here.
# NEWLINE is a sel_item so a big array literal can be laid out over several lines.
selector    = '[' sel_item* ']' axistuple?
sel_item    = NEWLINE | sub_array | sel_word
sub_array   = '[' sel_item* ']'                # nested axis of an array literal
sel_word    = NUMWORD | KVWORD | WORD | PARENWORD
axistuple   = PARENWORD                        # the sample call: `(u)`, `(u,v)`
block       = '{' body_item* '}'
